  <article class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-12">
    <header class="mb-10">
      <div class="inline-block bg-indigo-100 text-indigo-800 px-4 py-1 rounded-full text-sm font-bold mb-4 border border-indigo-200 shadow-sm">
        Occupation Guide
      </div>
      <h1 class="text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-4 leading-tight">
        BC PNP for Nurses and Health Care Assistants in 2027: Registration, Timelines and the Best Stream
      </h1>
      <p class="text-xl text-gray-600 mb-4">
        For nurses and health care assistants, the hard part of B.C. immigration is usually not the BC PNP score. It is getting registered to practise, and then choosing the right stream. Here is how each piece works in late 2026.
      </p>
      <div class="flex items-center gap-4 text-sm text-gray-500">
        <time datetime="2026-10-14">October 14, 2026</time>
        <span>&bull;</span>
        <span>13 min read</span>
      </div>
    </header>

    <div class="grid sm:grid-cols-3 gap-4 mb-10">
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-indigo-600 mb-2">1,228</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Nurses and NPs nominated by the BC PNP since 2022</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-green-600 mb-2">76</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Health draw minimum score, Sept 10 and Oct 8, 2026</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-red-600 mb-2">699</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Invitations in six 2026 Health draws</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
        Health care sits at the top of the BC PNP's Care objective. The program's April 23, 2026 announcement reported that it has nominated 3,887 health care professionals since 2022, including 1,228 nurses and nurse practitioners. Registered nurses (NOC 31301), licensed practical nurses (32101) and nurse aides, orderlies and patient service associates (33102, which includes health care assistants) are all on the 31-occupation Care health list. But the BC PNP will only nominate you for a job you are qualified to do, so the route always starts with your regulator.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Step one for nurses: BCCNM registration</h2>

      <p>
        The British Columbia College of Nurses and Midwives (BCCNM) licenses RNs, LPNs, psychiatric nurses and nurse practitioners. If you trained outside Canada, BCCNM's current process looks like this:
      </p>

      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Apply to Inspire Global Assessments and to BCCNM at the same time.</strong> Inspire confirms your identity, evaluates your English and education credentials, and runs a competency assessment of your nursing knowledge and skills, then sends the results to BCCNM.</li>
        <li><strong>Verification.</strong> BCCNM confirms your past practice and licences. Employers and regulators must send the forms directly to BCCNM, in English.</li>
        <li><strong>Assessment.</strong> BCCNM decides whether your education and practice are substantially equivalent to a B.C. graduate. Most applicants are assessed for both LPN and RN licensure in one application.</li>
        <li><strong>Any required education.</strong> This can range from a single course on the Canadian health system to a full re-entry or bridging program. Long gaps in practice make a bigger requirement more likely; BCCNM says applicants are more likely to succeed if they graduated or practised consistently within the last 10 years.</li>
        <li><strong>Exam.</strong> The NCLEX-RN for RNs or the REx-PN for LPNs. Optional provisional registration is available once everything except the exam is done.</li>
        <li><strong>Practising licence.</strong> You may not practise until you appear in BCCNM's online directory.</li>
      </ol>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Faster route for Australia, New Zealand and the UK</h3>

      <p>
        RNs (Adult or Child) educated in Australia, New Zealand or the United Kingdom have an expedited path: Inspire verifies identity, English and education; you complete one of two approved courses (at KPU or BCIT) on nursing in Canada; you then receive a provisional licence and complete 250 hours of transitional practice with one employer within a year; and you write the NCLEX-RN. BCCNM also has a separate route for U.S.-educated nurses.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">English, costs and what changed from NNAS</h3>

      <p>
        BCCNM's approved English tests and minimums for RNs include CELBAN (reading 8, listening 9, writing 7, speaking 8), IELTS Academic (overall 7.0, with reading 6.5, listening 7.0, writing 6.5, speaking 7.0), OET and PTE Academic. Evidence must be no more than two years old. Note these are different tests from the BC PNP's (which accepts IELTS General Training, CELPIP-General, PTE Core, TEF Canada or TCF Canada), so you may need two tests.
      </p>

      <p>
        Until 2023, credential checks went through the National Nursing Assessment Service (NNAS), which the B.C. government said could take up to two years. In January 2023 the province introduced a new pathway, said internationally educated nurses would no longer pay more than $3,700 in application and assessment fees upfront, and estimated the wait for registration would drop from three years to about four to nine months. That estimate is from 2023, so treat it as a guide, not a promise. Today BCCNM says "the majority of fees are available for reimbursement through the Internationally Educated Nurse Bursary Program," and BCCNM warns against paying agencies to submit applications for you. It also recommends having your work permit in place before you apply.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Step one for HCAs: the BC Care Aide &amp; Community Health Worker Registry</h2>

      <p>
        The Registry says HCAs must be registered to work in public health care settings in B.C., and the BC PNP requires registration for any NOC 33102 job offer, whether the employer is public or private. How you register depends on where you trained:
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Your training</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Registry category</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">What to expect</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Recognized B.C. HCA program</td><td class="px-4 py-3">B.C. graduate</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Application review takes 5–10 business days</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">HCA program in another province</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Canadian graduate</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Two pathways with different requirements</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">HCA-equivalent or nursing program abroad</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Internationally educated</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Credential report, English test, Inspire competency assessment</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>For internationally educated applicants, the Registry's process is:</p>

      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li>Apply online with proof of training, an English test (IELTS Academic, CELBAN, PTE Academic or OET; native English speakers use an exemption form) and an ICES Basic Evaluation Report or an accepted equivalent such as NNAS, WES or IQAS.</li>
        <li>The Registry contacts you within 10 business days and, if you qualify, sends a referral consent form.</li>
        <li>Inspire Global Assessments emails scheduling and payment instructions in about 15 business days and sends your performance report within 60 days of the assessment.</li>
        <li>The Registry reviews the report within 15 business days. You may need remedial education before registration; if all competencies are demonstrated, you are registered.</li>
      </ol>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Which BC PNP stream: Health Authority or Skilled Worker?</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700"></th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Health Authority stream</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Skilled Worker stream (targeted Health draw)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Employer</td><td class="px-4 py-3">A B.C. public health authority, as a direct employee</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Any eligible B.C. employer, e.g. private long-term care homes or clinics</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Registration and draw</td><td class="px-4 py-3">None. Apply directly once supported</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Register, wait for a Health draw, apply within 30 days</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Occupations</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Any NOC starting with 3, plus 41300, 41301, 42201</td><td class="px-4 py-3">The 31 Care health NOCs for targeted invitations</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Work experience</td><td class="px-4 py-3">As required by the BC PNP and your health authority</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Two years full-time in any TEER 0–3 job within 10 years</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Key hurdle</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Health authority support, signed only by delegated staff</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Reaching the draw's minimum score</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">EEBC option</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Yes</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Yes</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        The health authorities are Provincial Health Services Authority, First Nations Health Authority, Fraser Health, Interior Health, Island Health, Northern Health, Vancouver Coastal Health and Providence Health Care. Each decides for itself which applications to support, and the job must be regular, indeterminate and full-time. The program guide notes that BC Health Careers offers free recruitment help on the health authorities' behalf. Our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-health-authority-stream" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Health Authority stream guide</a> explains the support process.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 p-6 my-8">
        <p class="font-semibold text-blue-900 mb-2">Not for nurses or HCAs: the rural/remote initiative</p>
        <p class="text-blue-800">The Temporary Rural/Remote Health Support Initiative, with up to 250 nominations, is only for health authority employees in cleaning and security jobs (NOC 64410, 65310, 65312) in rural or remote areas. Its registration closed October 7, 2026.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Every 2026 targeted Health draw</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Date</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Minimum score</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Invitations</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">May 6, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">108</td><td class="px-4 py-3">117</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">June 2, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">100</td><td class="px-4 py-3">117</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">July 9, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">96</td><td class="px-4 py-3">116</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">August 6, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">84</td><td class="px-4 py-3">124</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">September 10, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">76</td><td class="px-4 py-3">143</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">October 8, 2026</td><td class="px-4 py-3">76</td><td class="px-4 py-3">82</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Source: WelcomeBC invitations to apply page. Each draw covered "all priority health care occupations."</p>

      <p>
        A 32-point drop in five months is significant. It suggests the pool of registered health workers with job offers was smaller than the invitations B.C. wanted to issue. Remember that Health Authority stream applicants are not in these draws at all, so the draw cutoffs describe only the private-employer side of the sector.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What you will earn and how it scores</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Occupation (Job Bank, B.C.)</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Low</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Median</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">High</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">BC PNP wage points at median</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Registered nurse (31301)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$35.00</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$47.58</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$57.00</td><td class="px-4 py-3">32</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Health care aide (33102)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$22.37</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$27.89</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$29.83</td><td class="px-4 py-3">12</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-sm text-slate-600">Hourly wages updated by Job Bank on November 19, 2025 (2023–2024 reference period).</p>

      <p>
        One quirk of the scoring grid: the BC PNP's professional designation list gives 5 points to <strong>LPNs licensed with BCCNM</strong> and to <strong>HCAs on the Care Aide Registry</strong>, but registered nurses are not on that list. Language also behaves differently: HCA jobs are TEER 3, so you need at least CLB 4; RN jobs are TEER 1, so a test is not mandatory at registration unless you claim language points (and you almost always should).
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Example 1: RN at a private care home in Kamloops</h3>
      <p>
        Wage of $45.00 an hour (30), five years of nursing experience (20) plus a year in Canada (10) and current employment with the employer (10), a foreign bachelor's degree (15), CLB 8 (25), Area 3 (15) and regional experience (10): <strong>135 points</strong>, far above recent cutoffs. For most qualified RNs, the score is not the obstacle; licensing and the job offer are.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Example 2: Registered HCA in Surrey vs Vernon</h3>
      <p>
        An HCA in a Surrey private care home earning $27.50 (12), with two years of experience (8), a year in Canada (10), current employment (10), a B.C. HCA certificate (5 + 8), Registry designation (5) and CLB 6 (15) scores <strong>73</strong> in Area 1, just under the 76 cutoff. The same profile working in Vernon for at least a year scores <strong>98</strong> (Area 3 plus regional experience). If the Surrey HCA worked directly for Fraser Health instead, the Health Authority stream would avoid the draw entirely. You can model these trade-offs with our <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-indigo-600 underline font-semibold">BC PNP calculator</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">A realistic timeline</h2>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Stage</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Published timing</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">HCA Registry: Inspire performance report</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Within 60 days of the assessment</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Nurses: BCCNM registration</td><td class="px-4 py-3">About 4–9 months (B.C.'s 2023 estimate); longer if bridging is required</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">BC PNP registration in pool</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Up to 12 months; Health draws were roughly monthly from May 2026</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">BC PNP application after invitation</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Submit within 30 days; about 80% decided within 3 months</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">IRCC permanent residence</td><td class="px-4 py-3">Separate federal processing; faster with EEBC</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Express Entry as a second door</h2>
      <p>
        RNs (31301), LPNs (32101) and nurse aides (33102) are all in IRCC's 2026 healthcare and social services category, which needs 12 months of experience in one eligible occupation within three years. IRCC held three such rounds through October 13, 2026, with cutoffs of 467 to 475. If you qualify for a federal program, using the BC PNP's EEBC option lets you pursue both doors at once. IRCC is reviewing its categories for 2027 and has floated narrowing them, possibly toward TEER 0–2 jobs, which would matter for HCAs. See <a href="/articles/express-entry-2027-category-changes" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">Express Entry 2027 category changes</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What to expect in 2027 (analysis)</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Health care should stay central.</strong> It is the largest group on B.C.'s Care list and the Health Authority stream continues. We expect targeted Health draws in 2027, but B.C. has not said so.</li>
        <li><strong>Cutoffs depend on the allocation.</strong> B.C. used a 2026 allocation of 6,254 after an August top-up. The 2027 number will follow the federal levels plan expected in early November 2026.</li>
        <li><strong>Licensing remains the bottleneck.</strong> Time spent on BCCNM or Registry requirements now is the most valuable preparation for 2027.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Common mistakes</h2>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Registering as an HCA before you are on the Registry.</strong> You will not receive a targeted invitation, and a 33102 job offer without registration is ineligible.</li>
        <li><strong>Accepting a casual or part-time health authority position.</strong> Both streams require full-time, indeterminate employment.</li>
        <li><strong>Paying an agency to file your BCCNM application.</strong> BCCNM says you must complete and submit your own application.</li>
        <li><strong>Using the wrong language test.</strong> BCCNM and the Registry accept academic or health-specific tests; the BC PNP accepts general tests. Plan for both if needed.</li>
        <li><strong>Claiming RN points with an LPN job offer.</strong> Your NOC follows the job you are offered, not your highest licence.</li>
      </ul>

